# Typed RPC records

Odroe can generate the wire codec for a project-local named-record typedef. A
record stays a Dart record in application code and becomes a JSON object only
at the RPC boundary. No annotation, `build_runner`, `toJson`, or global adapter
registry is involved.

This is the smallest useful domain model for the generated full-stack product:

```dart
// lib/posts.dart
typedef Post = ({int id, String title});
typedef CreatePost = ({String title});
```

Keep that file client-safe. It must not import SQLite, D1, `dart:io`, a server
host, or application secrets. Flutter and the server import the same types.

## Import the shared model with a prefix

A route server file imports the model with an explicit prefix. The prefix tells
the route compiler which library owns the client-visible type and prevents a
server-only declaration from leaking into generated client code.

The typedef must be declared directly in that imported source file. The current
compiler does not follow `part` directives or re-exports to discover it. If
`posts.dart` only exports `post_models.dart`, import `post_models.dart` itself
with the prefix instead.

```dart
import 'package:odroe/database.dart';
import 'package:odroe/server.dart';

import '../../posts.dart' as models;
import '../../posts_database.dart';
import 'route.dart' as definition;

final route = definition.route.server();

final listPosts = ServerFunction<String, List<models.Post>>(
  id: 'posts.list',
  method: HttpMethod.get,
  handler: (context) {
    final order = switch (context.data) {
      'newest' => posts.id.descending,
      'oldest' => posts.id.ascending,
      _ => throw const HttpError(400, 'Invalid post sort.'),
    };
    return postQueries
        .selectTable(posts, orderBy: <SqlOrder>[order])
        .all(context.request.read(databaseKey));
  },
);

final createPost = ServerFunction<models.CreatePost, models.Post>(
  id: 'posts.create',
  method: HttpMethod.post,
  handler: (context) {
    final title = context.data.title.trim();
    if (title.isEmpty) {
      throw const HttpError(400, 'Post title is required.');
    }
    return postQueries
        .insert(posts, <SqlAssignment>[posts.title.set(title)])
        .returning(posts.projection)
        .one(context.request.read(databaseKey));
  },
);
```

`odroe generate` resolves `models.Post` and `models.CreatePost`, reads their
named fields, and emits the four transformations required by one typed call:

```text
Flutter record
  → generated input encoder
  → JSON object
  → generated server input decoder
  → handler record

handler record
  → generated server output encoder
  → JSON object
  → generated client output decoder
  → Flutter record
```

The same output codec is applied to each stream item. Collection codecs recurse,
so `List<Post>` remains `List<Post>` on both sides instead of becoming
`List<dynamic>` or failing as an unregistered `Record` at runtime.

## Call records directly from Flutter

Generated references expose the original Dart types:

```dart
final created = await generated.routes.posts.createPost(
  rpc,
  (title: 'Typed from Flutter'),
);

final posts = await generated.routes.posts.listPosts(rpc, 'newest');

print(created.id);
print(posts.first.title);
```

There is no application-owned map or cast in this path. A malformed object is
rejected before the server handler starts. A successful response whose shape
does not match the generated output contract becomes `RpcProtocolException` on
the client instead of exposing an incidental cast error.

## Know the supported boundary

Generated record codecs intentionally cover a narrow, inspectable shape:

- a project-local typedef declared directly in the same source file that the
  route server imports with a prefix; `part` and re-export discovery are not
  supported;
- named fields only;
- non-generic record aliases;
- use-site and field nullability, plus `List`, `Set`, `Iterable`, and
  `Map<String, T>` around supported values;
- scalar values already supported by `Serializer`, including `DateTime`,
  `Duration`, `Uri`, `BigInt`, and `Uint8List`.

Positional records, generic record aliases, recursive aliases, function values,
and maps with non-string keys are rejected during route generation. This
same-file record rule does not add generated support for a nominal class,
including one declared beside the typedef. Nominal classes remain
application-owned and use `SerializationAdapter` when they must cross the wire;
Odroe does not infer constructors or generate `fromJson` for classes.

Put `?` on the function use site or a record field, such as `models.Post?`.
A typedef whose record target is itself nullable is rejected because that alias
cannot name the promoted non-null record needed by generated field access.

## Treat the fields as a protocol

The function ID and record field names are wire contracts. Renaming a field,
changing its type, or changing nullability can break an older client even when
the Dart typedef still looks small. Use a new function ID or a coordinated
client/server release for a breaking shape change.

The generated wire value is an object keyed by field name. Its key set must
match the record exactly: missing fields and unknown fields are rejected, even
when a missing field would otherwise be nullable. That fail-closed rule prevents
two deployed shapes from drifting silently. Do not depend on field order and do
not place secrets in a record merely because the type is shared. Payload and
response-frame byte limits still apply after encoding; use pagination or
streaming for large collections.

Run the compiler explicitly after changing a shared record:

```sh
dart run odroe generate
dart analyze --fatal-infos
```

